{% import "v2/macros.njk" as m %}
{% set app = apps | bySlug('weatherus') %}
{% set playUrl = 'https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=' + app.packageName %}
{% set shots = '/assets/images/weatherus/screenshots/' %}

{{ m.appHero({
  app: app, lang: 'en',
  category: 'Weather & Road Live', tags: ['Android · iOS', 'Free'],
  title: 'Weather Radar USA',
  lead: 'Open it and you are already looking at an animated 2-hour radar loop; one tap switches to live GOES satellite imagery. National Weather Service watches and warnings sit on the same map, and tapping any point reads out the full alert.',
  playUrl: playUrl, appStoreUrl: app.appStoreUrl,
  facts: ['No sign-up', 'Location optional', 'Phones and tablets', 'English'],
  shots: [
    { src: shots + '01_radar.webp', w: 960, h: 2080 },
    { src: shots + '04_satellite.webp', w: 960, h: 2080 }
  ]
}) }}

{{ m.facts([
  { num: '2 hours', label: 'Radar loop · 12 frames at 10-minute steps' },
  { num: 'Full text', label: 'NWS watches and warnings, as issued' },
  { num: '9', label: 'GOES imagery channels' },
  { num: '22', label: 'Regions · CONUS, Alaska, Hawaii, Puerto Rico, full disk' }
]) }}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Radar', title: 'The map answers first,<br>the menu comes later',
  shots: [ { src: shots + '01_radar.webp', alt: 'Radar map — animated NEXRAD loop over Louisiana with alerts-nearby chip and playback bar', w: 960, h: 2080 } ] }) %}
  <p>Most of the time the question is simple: is it raining on me, and is it about to? The app opens straight onto the radar map where you left it, and fills in the last two hours of echoes while the map is still settling. No splash screen, no sign-in.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>12 frames at 10-minute steps</strong> from the NOAA MRMS reflectivity mosaic</li>
    <li>Playback <strong>keeps running while you pan and zoom</strong></li>
    <li>A <strong>dark, desaturated base map</strong> so the storm is the only bright thing on screen</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Warnings', title: 'Tap the map,<br>read the whole alert', soft: true, flip: true,
  shots: [
    { src: shots + '02_hazards.webp', alt: 'Warnings mode — National Weather Service watch and warning polygons across the Mississippi valley', w: 960, h: 2080 },
    { src: shots + '03_hazard_sheet.webp', alt: 'Alert detail sheet — Tropical Storm Warning with what, where, when and safety instructions', w: 960, h: 2080 }
  ] }) %}
  <p>Warnings mode draws every active National Weather Service alert polygon — severe thunderstorm, tornado, flood, winter storm, heat, marine. Tap any point and the sheet shows the alert text as issued, not a summary. This is the part most radar apps leave out.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>WHAT / WHERE / WHEN / IMPACTS</strong>, then the precautionary instructions</li>
    <li>Overlapping alerts at the tapped point <strong>all appear in the sheet</strong></li>
    <li>An <strong>alerts nearby</strong> chip on the radar side, red when one is a warning</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Satellite', title: 'Live GOES imagery,<br>one tap away',
  shots: [ { src: shots + '04_satellite.webp', alt: 'Satellite screen — GOES-19 East GeoColor imagery of the continental United States', w: 960, h: 2080 } ] }) %}
  <p>Where radar tells you what is falling right now, satellite tells you what is on the way — a frontal band, the spin of a tropical system, a line of storms still a state away.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>Current loops from <strong>GOES East and GOES West</strong>, as a 2-hour animation</li>
    <li>The image <strong>fills the entire screen</strong>, with controls floating on a soft scrim</li>
    <li>Set radar or satellite as the <strong>start screen</strong></li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Zoom', title: 'Zoom in on any storm,<br>keep it moving', soft: true, flip: true,
  shots: [ { src: shots + '05_zoom.webp', alt: 'Zoomed satellite imagery — pinch and double tap to inspect cloud structure', w: 960, h: 2080 } ] }) %}
  <p>Zoom survives the loop. Frames keep advancing at whatever magnification you set — the whole point when you are watching a single cell rotate.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>Pinch</strong> freely, <strong>double tap</strong> to step in 2x, <strong>flick</strong> with real inertia</li>
    <li>Any edge or corner can be dragged to the <strong>center of the screen</strong></li>
    <li>Pinch past the fit boundary and let go to <strong>snap back</strong> to the full view</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Channels · Regions', title: '9 channels,<br>22 regions',
  shots: [
    { src: shots + '06_channels.webp', alt: 'Channel picker — GeoColor, infrared, water vapor, visible, air mass, sandwich, dust, fire temperature, day/night cloud', w: 960, h: 2080 },
    { src: shots + '07_regions.webp', alt: 'Region picker — search by state, my location, and a map of available sectors', w: 960, h: 2080 }
  ] }) %}
  <p>The channel picker is a grid of live thumbnails, so you pick by what the image looks like rather than by a spectral band. Coverage follows NOAA's own sectors, and the picker also takes a state name.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>GeoColor</strong> by default — true color by day, infrared with city lights at night</li>
    <li>Type <strong>Texas</strong> and it takes you to the sector that covers Texas</li>
    <li>A <strong>My location</strong> shortcut picks the nearest sector</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.more('See all features', 'Channels · Regions · Radar details · Settings') %}
  <div>
    <h4>9 imagery channels</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>GeoColor — true-color by day, infrared blend with city lights at night</li>
      <li>Infrared — cloud-top temperature, around the clock, picks out the tallest storm tops</li>
      <li>Water vapor — mid-atmosphere moisture, where the jet stream and dry slots show</li>
      <li>Visible — plain reflected sunlight, sharpest daytime detail</li>
      <li>Air mass, Sandwich, Day/night cloud — composites that separate air masses and cloud types by color</li>
      <li>Dust and Fire temperature — blowing dust events and active fire hot spots</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h4>22 regions</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>The continental US, nine mainland regions, Alaska, Hawaii, Puerto Rico and the Caribbean, and full-disk views from both satellites</li>
      <li>A tappable map, a list of wide-area views, and state-name search</li>
      <li>The region list comes from the server, so the app follows when NOAA reshuffles a sector</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h4>Radar details</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>The timestamp under the map is the frame's own valid time, plus how long ago that was</li>
      <li>The color legend is one tap away and stays collapsed until you want it</li>
      <li>Radar and Warnings are exclusive modes so both stay instant when you change zoom</li>
      <li>Without location permission, the alerts chip uses the map center instead of asking again</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h4>Simple by design</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>No account, no sign up — install it and you are looking at radar</li>
      <li>Start screen: radar, satellite, or whichever you saw last (default)</li>
      <li>Image quality setting for slower connections</li>
      <li>Phones and tablets, portrait and landscape — rotating keeps the spot you were looking at</li>
      <li>The map remembers where you left it, with that area's tiles already cached</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
{% endcall %}

